---
import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
import ModellingVisual from '@/components/modelling/ModellingVisual.astro';
import DetailDisclosure from '@/components/modelling/DetailDisclosure.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="A property story, carefully connected" description="Follow Harbour Court from an inspection to a corrected report. See how a small set of distinctions makes the story easier to read, question and improve." chapter="Understand · The worked case" claim="example">
  <p>
    Harbour Court at 14 Orchard Road is fictional. Its building contains Flat 1 and
    Flat 2. The examples below teach modelling distinctions; they are not records
    from the Property Pack candidate, legal advice or current conformance results.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="act-one">
    <h2 id="act-one"><span id="identifiers" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="classes-properties" aria-hidden="true"></span>1. Establish the cast before joining the dots</h2>
    <p>
      A message says “the property has been inspected”. Before adding a date, ask
      what “the property” identifies. Was the inspection about Flat 1, the whole
      building or both? Does the report itself make the scope clear? Our example
      treats the inspection as concerning Flat 1; that is an explicit teaching
      assumption, not something inferred from the street address.
    </p>
    <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="cast-caption">
      <figcaption id="cast-caption">Related subjects are not interchangeable identities</figcaption>
      <ModellingVisual kind="identity-map" />
      <dl>
        <dt>Building</dt><dd>Harbour Court contains the two dwellings.</dd>
        <dt>Dwelling</dt><dd>Flat 1 is the subject of this illustrative inspection; Flat 2 is not silently included.</dd>
        <dt>Address description</dt><dd>A description used to refer to Flat 1 at 14 Orchard Road.</dd>
        <dt>Title record</dt><dd>A separately identified record. Its exact relationship to the dwellings needs evidence; the address alone does not establish it.</dd>
        <dt>Inspection</dt><dd>An event, distinct from each report describing it.</dd>
      </dl>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Read a connection as a sentence: “the building contains Flat 1” or “the report
      describes the inspection”. A line without a clear relationship name asks the
      reader to guess. If the sentence overstates what the evidence says, the model
      needs a narrower statement or an unresolved question.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="act-two">
    <h2 id="act-two">2. Give the dates the right subjects</h2>
    <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="timeline-caption">
      <figcaption id="timeline-caption">One inspection, two report versions</figcaption>
      <ModellingVisual kind="evidence-timeline" />
      <ol class="modelling-steps">
        <li><h3><time datetime="2026-08-12">12 August 2026</time> · Inspection</h3><p>The inspection of Flat 1 occurs. This is the event date in our scenario.</p></li>
        <li><h3><time datetime="2026-08-14">14 August 2026</time> · Report version 1</h3><p>The first report is issued and describes the 12 August inspection.</p></li>
        <li><h3><time datetime="2026-08-20">20 August 2026</time> · Report version 2</h3><p>A correction is issued. It revises version 1; it does not describe a new inspection in this scenario.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Keeping the earlier report identifiable lets somebody explain which version
      supported a decision at the time. The corrected version does not need to erase
      that history. Conversely, retaining history does not mean presenting superseded
      wording as if it were the current account.
    </p>
    <p>
      There can be other clocks. A system might record the report later, and a claim
      might apply only during a stated period. Neither the recording date nor report
      issue date should stand in for a claim's period of applicability. This case
      supplies no such period; the honest value is “not established here”.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Review exercise: “Version 2 means a second inspection”</summary>
      <p>That conclusion does not follow. Here the correction produces a new report version, while the inspection stays the same event. If another visit actually occurred, the evidence would need to identify it separately. A count of documents is not a count of inspections.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="act-three">
    <h2 id="act-three"><span id="shapes-provenance" aria-hidden="true"></span>3. Follow a claim back to its evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose a claim says that an inspection of Flat 1 occurred on 12 August. The
      claim is an assertion somebody made; the inspection is the event it describes;
      the report is evidence offered in support. Treating these as three distinct
      subjects allows the recipient to ask who made the claim, which report version
      supports it and whether the correction affects it.
    </p>
    <div class="modelling-comparison">
      <section aria-labelledby="unchanged-claim">
        <h3 id="unchanged-claim">The correction leaves the date unchanged</h3>
        <p>The claim may still be supported. Its connection to the corrected evidence should be reviewed, not assumed merely because the same address appears.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="changed-claim">
        <h3 id="changed-claim">The correction changes something the claim relied on</h3>
        <p>The claim needs another look. Preserve the previous source relationship so the reason for the change remains understandable.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p>
      We have deliberately not invented the contents of a survey finding or a rule
      about acceptable evidence. The example demonstrates the questions a model can
      support. Domain participants must establish the actual meaning and evidential
      requirements for their context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="act-four">
    <h2 id="act-four">4. A role needs its setting</h2>
    <p>
      A person can take part as a buyer in one transaction and as a seller in another.
      Those descriptions concern participation; they are not two incompatible kinds
      of person. In the Harbour Court case, we keep the person and the transaction
      separately identifiable so a role can be understood in its setting.
    </p>
    <p>
      An open review question is enough: “Which transaction makes this person the
      buyer here, and what time or other qualification is needed?” Participants do
      not have to decide how that role should be encoded. They do need to notice
      when the model loses the setting that makes the statement meaningful.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="act-five">
    <h2 id="act-five">5. Let a hard case expose what is not decided</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine Flat 1 is later subdivided. Does the original dwelling persist, do new
      dwellings replace it, or does the answer depend on which kind of resource is
      being tracked? The inspection remains a historical event, but deciding how its
      subject relates to later dwellings needs more than a matching address string.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>What should a careful model record before resolving subdivision?</summary>
      <p>State which resource's identity is being considered; record the relevant dates and evidence; explain the possible interpretations; and identify who must review them. Do not merge the building, dwelling and title record to remove the uncertainty. The identity criterion is unresolved in this teaching case.</p>
    </details>
    <p>
      A visible unresolved question is useful information. It tells implementers not
      to rely on an unstated equivalence and tells participants exactly where their
      expertise can improve the candidate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <DetailDisclosure summary="Stable illustrative identifiers" note="Optional technical view">
    <p>These example.org identifiers are teaching references, not OPDA vocabulary terms or promised live resource pages.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Building</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/building</code></dd>
      <dt>Flat 1</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/dwelling-1</code></dd>
      <dt>Address description</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/address-1</code></dd>
      <dt>Title record</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/title-record</code></dd>
      <dt>Inspection</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/inspection</code></dd>
      <dt>Report versions</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/report-v1</code> and <code>https://example.org/harbour-court/report-v2</code></dd>
      <dt>Claim</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/claim-1</code></dd>
      <dt>Participant and transaction</dt><dd><code>https://example.org/harbour-court/person-1</code> and <code>https://example.org/harbour-court/transaction</code></dd>
    </dl>
    <p>Identifiers do not, by themselves, establish identity criteria. In particular, this case makes no <code>owl:sameAs</code> assertion between the building and title record.</p>
  </DetailDisclosure>

  <section aria-labelledby="use-the-story">
    <h2 id="use-the-story"><span id="vocabularies" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="property-pack-walkthrough" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="read-pages" aria-hidden="true"></span>Use the story on a real model page</h2>
    <p>
      Open a page in the <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack candidate</a>
      or use <a href="/search">site search</a>. Read its own status and definition;
      do not assume it implements this fictional example. Then ask: which thing is
      being described, what does one relationship mean, and what exception would
      challenge it? Keep the page link with your observation.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-a-definition">Turn an observation into useful feedback →</a> or <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/things-and-identities">explore identity in more depth</a>.</p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
